**My revised first choice is a native algorithm for the exact point count of one fixed elliptic curve over \(\mathbb Z/N\mathbb Z\).** It has one missing algorithmic step, computing the count from \(N\). The repo already has a randomized recovery procedure from that count for every good semiprime. The single-coefficient proposal has two missing steps. I retract the 37a1 scalar probe and the random-matrix probe. [Point-count reduction, Theorem EXT](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:24)

## Q1. A narrower mixed-arithmetic route

I would reduce the original 32% to **16% for adaptive factor-interval refinement**. The structural requirement is that a fixed, efficiently computable rule takes \(N\) and a candidate interval for \(p\), scores its children, and keeps the true factor among polynomially many surviving intervals. Starting from the public balanced range, it must reach width below \(N^{1/4}\), where Coppersmith finishes. This specifies what each round gains, how errors compound, and what counts as recovery. Even 60% accuracy on isolated branch decisions could lose the true path exponentially often.

The entropy experiment already tested the substance of my nested-power bit probe. Its five confirmed signals came from continued fractions and decayed approximately as \(N^{-1/4}\). I see no reason to rerun that search. A test conditioned on a *candidate interval*, with an algebraic prediction about which child contains a divisor, asks a different question. [Entropy results](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/entropy_results.md) · [Known-bits frontier](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md:279)

## Q2. Which matrix words survive?

- **Fixed nontrivial word, independent uniform matrices:** The Larsen–Shalev bound kills the proposal. Identity in \(\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb F_p)\) implies identity in its simple quotient \(\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb F_p)\). The probability is at most \(|G|^{-c(w)}\) for large \(G\). On balanced \(N=pq\), even the sum of the two local hit probabilities is exponentially small in the input length. [Larsen–Shalev](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2011.10.040)
- **A word chosen from \(N\), then tested on fresh uniform matrices:** The fixed-word theorem alone leaves a quantifier gap because \(c\) depends on the word. In rank two, the polynomial zero-count argument closes it for **expanded** length \(\ell=\mathrm{poly}(\log N)\). On the standard matrix coordinate chart, clearing denominators gives a nonzero polynomial of degree \(O(\ell)\). The probability of a zero, including the cost of missing the chart, is \(O(\ell/p)\). Conditioning on \(N\) fixes the word before the matrices are sampled. [Gamburd et al., §4.1](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0707.1833) · [Hadad, Lemma 3.6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0808.0622)
- **Fixed small integer matrices:** For an \(N\)-independent expanded word, evaluate it over the integers. If the result is \(I\), both components hit. Otherwise a nonzero entry of \(w-I\) has \(O(\ell)\) bits and hence only \(O(\ell/\log p)\) prime divisors of size \(p\). Structured inputs chosen *from \(N\)* escape this counting argument. So do programs whose repeated squarings or commutators give exponential expanded length. Either needs a reason its output vanishes at one hidden prime often. I have none for the proposed commutator.

## Q3. What the single coefficient actually supplies

For 37a1, \(A=a_pa_q\). I had no divisibility argument linking \(N+1-A\) to the order of \((0,0)\) at either prime. The scalar probe had no justified signal.

Bach–Charles illustrates exactly what a recovery mechanism would need. In weight two, the additional value \(a_{N^2}\) gives
\[
S=A^2+N-a_{N^2}=q a_p^2+p a_q^2.
\]
Thus \(q a_p^2\) and \(p a_q^2\) are the roots of \(z^2-Sz+NA^2\). These quantities carry the hidden primes explicitly, enabling recovery on a density-one set. From \((N,A)\) alone, the plausible research target is a cheap operation on the curve or its torsion that supplies comparable information involving \(p\) and \(q\) separately. I know no such operation. By contrast, the actual count \(M=(p+1-a_p)(q+1-a_q)\) genuinely annihilates curve points, and Theorem EXT arranges a component on which annihilation fails. [Bach–Charles](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192) · [Trace identities and EXT](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md:154)

## Q4–Q5. Revised weights

These are subjective weights conditional on classical polynomial-time factoring. I assign the weight to the step that delivers recovery.

| Weight | Location | Decisive consideration |
|---:|---|---|
| **24%** | **Native fixed-curve point count** | A uniform evaluator for \(M\bmod\ell\) at enough small primes reconstructs \(M\) by CRT. The obstacle is computing those residues at composite \(N\). Schoof’s prime-field Frobenius step has no direct composite analogue. [Native modular methods](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nativemod_results.md) |
| 20% | Unidentified mechanism | The catalogue has no theorem covering arbitrary compositions. [Completeness audit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/completeness_audit_results.md:149) |
| 16% | Adaptive factor-interval refinement | It has a verifiable recovery criterion. The pilot below lowers my confidence in its simplest lattice implementation. |
| 13% | Class numbers or regulators | For negative \(D=-4N\), precision sufficient to round the class-number formula gives exact \(h(D)\). With suitable class sampling, its 2-primary subgroup yields useful ambiguous forms. For positive discriminants, a certified regulator multiple such as \(hR\) reaches a midpoint under the norm hypothesis. Usefulness of that midpoint remains a separate condition. The \(N^{1/5}\) precision wall prices restricted linear estimators plus a square-root finish. [Genus and infrastructure](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genusleak_results.md:288) · [Precision theorem](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nativeana_results.md:25) |
| 12% | Constructive relations | Requires excess smoothness, enough exceptionally small independent norms, or a fruitful dependency from few fresh discoveries. [Three hatches](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lowerbound_results.md) |
| 8% | Fast modular factorial or dense arithmetic circuit | Uniform modular evaluation has a direct gcd finish. [Circuit models](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/slpcirc_results.md) |
| 4% | Structured groups | Includes \(N\)-dependent matrix inputs and higher-genus Jacobians. Jacobians are abelian. Raising the genus still leaves ordinary scalar annihilation reliant on favorable group orders. [Genus-two analysis](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/genus2rh_results.md) |
| 2% | Classical period finding | Theorem T requires a cheaply readable observable whose character contributions reinforce at a useful large-order frequency. The repo’s strong preprocessing construction would also solve prime-field discrete logs on the covered primes. [Theorem T](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/periodfind_results.md:120) · [DL transfer](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/prepread_results.md:40) |
| 1% | One supplied non-CM coefficient | Both native evaluation and single-value recovery remain open. [Single-coefficient audit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/cuspcoef_results.md:24) |

**The best cheap new probe I found was to score sibling intervals using the dual Coppersmith lattice. I ran it in scratch.** For a candidate interval centered at \(a\) with radius \(X\), put \(t=(p-a)/X\). Every polynomial in the ideal \((N,a+x)^m\) evaluates at \(p-a\) to a multiple of \(p^m\). Consequently the dual of its scaled coefficient lattice contains \(p^{-m}(1,t,t^2,\ldots)\). The correct child has \(|t|\le1\). This gives a specific vector shape to look for before an ordinary root certificate succeeds. The repo tested primal short vectors and unconditional bit readouts. [Top-block experiments](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md:569) · [Entropy runbook](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/experiments/entropy/round2/RUNBOOK.txt)

The pilot supplied the correct **parent** interval and hid which child contained \(p\). Across 48-, 64- and 80-bit inputs, the fixed eight-dimensional score chose correctly **216/216** times at radius approximately \(N^{0.12}\). At \(N^{0.28}\) it scored **111/216**, and at \(N^{0.34}\), **102/216**, against chance at 108. It found **zero factors in the 432 above-threshold trials**. The easy control supplied enough location information that either sibling center also allowed factor recovery. I would retire this score. The broader interval route would need a different invariant that preserves the true branch starting from a public interval. [Reproducible scratch pilot](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/inp_c/dual_interval_pilot.py)